If another reason, quite simply, why we should look at cities

233
00:15:25,320 --> 00:15:26,920
is that's where all the people are.

234
00:15:26,920 --> 00:15:30,640
This is the graph of the percentage of urban world population

235
00:15:30,640 --> 00:15:35,720
as a proportion of the total population of the UN World Urbanization prospects.

236
00:15:35,960 --> 00:15:37,880
You can see then every region in the world,

237
00:15:38,960 --> 00:15:42,680
if the blue is the world population and red is the urban population,

238
00:15:43,080 --> 00:15:46,120
every region of the world, Africa, Asia and Europe.

239
00:15:46,760 --> 00:15:50,440
Europe is already quite urbanized at 75 percent now,

240
00:15:50,440 --> 00:15:53,200
but it will become even more urbanized by 2050,

241
00:15:53,240 --> 00:15:56,520
which is our mid-century argument for decarbonization.

242
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:01,320
Asia is right at 50 percent, half and half between urban world population,

243
00:16:01,320 --> 00:16:04,640
but will become even more urban over the next 20 years.

244
00:16:04,640 --> 00:16:08,760
That'll be mostly urbanization happening in Southeast Asia or India.

245
00:16:09,240 --> 00:16:14,440
And Africa is 42 percent urbanized, but by 2050, it's going to be about 60 percent urbanized.

246
00:16:14,760 --> 00:16:17,880
That's mostly urbanization that's going to occur in Sub-Saharan Africa.

247
00:16:19,160 --> 00:16:22,480
If you look at Latin America and Caribbean already amazingly urbanized

248
00:16:22,480 --> 00:16:25,360
at 81 percent, North America at 82 percent,

249
00:16:25,600 --> 00:16:30,480
but both regions will be probably pushing 85 or 90 percent urbanized by 2050.

250
00:16:30,800 --> 00:16:33,440
And Oceania, which is Australian New Zealand

251
00:16:34,440 --> 00:16:38,320
and the Pacific Islands will probably stay roughly as urbanized as it is,

252
00:16:38,560 --> 00:16:41,160
which is already 68 percent.

253
00:16:41,160 --> 00:16:44,920
So the argument for focusing on cities is simply that's where all the people are.

254
00:16:44,920 --> 00:16:46,680
That's where all the economic activity is.

255
00:16:46,760 --> 00:16:49,240
That's what we should focus on to try to reduce our consumption.

256
00:16:49,920 --> 00:16:52,240
We're going to focus on U.S. cities in this class,

257
00:16:52,240 --> 00:16:55,560
partly simply because we're teaching in an American institution.

258
00:16:55,800 --> 00:16:59,320
Also, we've had recent climate developments, which are quite exciting to me.

259
00:16:59,560 --> 00:17:02,480
Here's an article by Marion Wasser at WBOR,

260
00:17:02,480 --> 00:17:05,960
just simply saying what we need to know about the new Massachusetts Climate Law

261
00:17:05,960 --> 00:17:09,000
that's going to affect our state energy policy quite a bit.

262
00:17:09,840 --> 00:17:13,320
This is a blog post from Amy Turner at Columbia University

263
00:17:13,320 --> 00:17:16,840
focusing on how cities will be affected by the Inflation Reduction Act,

264
00:17:16,880 --> 00:17:22,480
which is our first major piece of climate legislation ever passed by climate

265
00:17:22,480 --> 00:17:26,320
and by Congress ever since Congress learned about climate change

266
00:17:26,320 --> 00:17:28,720
and the problem the first time 30 years ago.

267
00:17:29,800 --> 00:17:32,760
And so I welcome insights and comparisons with other countries.

268
00:17:32,760 --> 00:17:36,360
I've done work in a number of other countries on energy and energy transition,

269
00:17:36,960 --> 00:17:39,840
but we're going to focus mostly in the past on U.S. cities

270
00:17:39,840 --> 00:17:43,840
simply because we have the moral responsibility to take climate action

271
00:17:44,120 --> 00:17:46,240
and also because they're studying in a U.S. institution.

272
00:17:46,240 --> 00:17:49,480
But again, I welcome your insights and comparisons with other countries.

273
00:17:49,800 --> 00:17:54,120
I'll try to bring in comparisons with other places I've worked or other regions

274
00:17:54,400 --> 00:17:58,880
as appropriate to try to illustrate some of these energy and climate issues

275
00:17:58,880 --> 00:18:01,040
and how they work in other cities around the world.

276
00:18:01,840 --> 00:18:05,560
And I also want to highlight that there's nothing in this class really should be off limits.

277
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:08,760
And I just want to emphasize that different people will get different value

278
00:18:08,760 --> 00:18:10,360
from different readings.

279
00:18:10,360 --> 00:18:14,440
For some people, Bill Gates was a ruthless monopolist in the 90s,

280
00:18:14,520 --> 00:18:20,640
a successful philanthropist in the aughts and the, let's say, early teens of the century.

281
00:18:20,920 --> 00:18:24,080
And you might have heard most recently, he had a terrible, terrible divorce

282
00:18:24,320 --> 00:18:26,600
and maybe his reputation somewhat damaged by that.

283
00:18:26,920 --> 00:18:30,520
But he's also known as somebody who's worked on child mortality

284
00:18:30,520 --> 00:18:33,640
and disease and climate change and pandemics.

285
00:18:33,720 --> 00:18:39,000
So you wrote an article in 2018 about climate change in the 75 percent problem

286
00:18:39,520 --> 00:18:43,480
arguing that we need new technologies and innovation to solve large parts

287
00:18:43,480 --> 00:18:46,440
of climate change that we don't already know how to solve.

288
00:18:47,320 --> 00:18:52,120
At the same time, we have this article by Nicola Jefferson and Leah Stokes,

289
00:18:52,120 --> 00:18:55,720
who are our graduates from DUSH and MIT, and this title

290
00:18:55,720 --> 00:18:58,080
are racist fossil fuel energy system.

291
00:18:58,480 --> 00:19:01,120
And I actually think it gives a number of good arguments

292
00:19:01,120 --> 00:19:05,680
why the fossil fuel energy system disproportionately affects black Americans.

293
00:19:06,120 --> 00:19:08,520
This is an article that was written in July 2020,

294
00:19:08,640 --> 00:19:11,200
right at the height of the Black Lives Matter protests.

295
00:19:11,760 --> 00:19:15,760
And it highlighted basically how the energy system affects

296
00:19:15,920 --> 00:19:18,520
different groups, especially black Americans, differently.

297
00:19:18,920 --> 00:19:21,320
Having said that, people have very different reactions.

298
00:19:21,320 --> 00:19:25,360
This is one student who came from the fossil fuel industry

299
00:19:25,760 --> 00:19:29,080
and she was quite offended by this article that she called

300
00:19:29,080 --> 00:19:32,920
our racist fossil fuel industry, which wasn't actually the title of the article,

301
00:19:33,200 --> 00:19:38,440
which just shows you how this label of labeling the energy system as racist.

302
00:19:38,760 --> 00:19:40,600
She took quite personally.

303
00:19:40,600 --> 00:19:43,280
And so I want to emphasize that you could look at both these articles

304
00:19:43,280 --> 00:19:47,280
by Bill Gates or Jefferson Stokes, and you can have different reactions to them.

305
00:19:47,480 --> 00:19:50,640
But what I want us to do is try to read lots of different viewpoints

306
00:19:50,840 --> 00:19:55,480
and engage with them and think through what we agree with or we don't agree with

307
00:19:55,600 --> 00:19:57,680
and use that to shape our viewpoints.

308
00:19:57,680 --> 00:20:00,720
And this is just to emphasize that the point of your education

309
00:20:00,720 --> 00:20:02,920
is to develop your own critically-reasoned viewpoint.

310
00:20:03,520 --> 00:20:05,840
But to develop your own critically-reasoned viewpoint,

311
00:20:05,840 --> 00:20:09,120
you have to engage with evidence to engage with other people's viewpoints

312
00:20:09,440 --> 00:20:10,760
and you have to engage with other people.

313
00:20:10,760 --> 00:20:15,200
So we're going to try to have nothing to be off limits in this class,

314
00:20:15,920 --> 00:20:20,960
even as we try to engage things rationally and respectfully,

315
00:20:21,240 --> 00:20:23,040
respectful of other people's viewpoints.

316
00:20:23,040 --> 00:20:26,640
I want us to try to keep on working and on developing our own critical reasoning

317
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:31,840
and trying to contrast and compare how other people see some of the same issues,

318
00:20:32,200 --> 00:20:34,120
even if we don't agree with them.

319
00:20:34,120 --> 00:20:37,520
And so in our class, I want to emphasize that we're going to learn question

320
00:20:37,520 --> 00:20:38,720
and reason together.

321
00:20:40,280 --> 00:20:44,880
Finally, I wrote this article this summer with a number of colleagues

322
00:20:45,240 --> 00:20:49,280
around the planning discipline, including Chris Egers, who's here at MIT.

323
00:20:49,760 --> 00:20:50,760
The article is titled,

324
00:20:50,760 --> 00:20:55,000
Planning the Built Environment and Land Use Towards Deep Decarbonization of the United States.

325
00:20:55,560 --> 00:20:59,080
And I wrote this article with my colleagues because we argue that,

326
00:20:59,280 --> 00:21:03,760
you know, deep decarbonization is basically our net zero goal for 2050.

327
00:21:04,240 --> 00:21:07,600
At the same time, we don't think that there's wide understanding

328
00:21:07,600 --> 00:21:10,680
of what we need to do with the built environment land use.

329
00:21:11,040 --> 00:21:14,480
And this is to say two things that the built environment land use

330
00:21:14,800 --> 00:21:18,040
are going to be critical to realizing our energy transition goals.

331
00:21:19,040 --> 00:21:22,360
And the same time, if we don't address the built environment and land use,

332
00:21:22,360 --> 00:21:24,240
we may not reach those goals.

333
00:21:24,240 --> 00:21:27,440
And we think that decarbonization has implications for cities

334
00:21:27,440 --> 00:21:30,800
and planning in terms of the scale of how we plan cities.

335
00:21:31,480 --> 00:21:33,680
The scope of what planners look at.

336
00:21:34,160 --> 00:21:36,800
The speed with which planning occurs.

337
00:21:36,800 --> 00:21:39,480
We need to achieve a decarbonization goal,

338
00:21:39,800 --> 00:21:43,440
which is basically changing our entire energy system 28 years from now.

339
00:21:43,600 --> 00:21:46,360
That's going to require planners to operate much faster.

340
00:21:46,960 --> 00:21:49,880
And we need to make sure that we remain relevant.

341
00:21:50,240 --> 00:21:54,280
If planners don't focus on mitigating greenhouse gas emissions,

342
00:21:54,520 --> 00:21:57,520
we will be left out of larger climate discussions

343
00:21:57,600 --> 00:22:00,360
happening in other disciplines or in government,

344
00:22:00,680 --> 00:22:03,840
which is much more focused on mitigation than adaptation.

345
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:06,160
But planners have to additionally focus on adaptation.

346
00:22:06,720 --> 00:22:10,800
And finally, there's an open question of what the role of land use

347
00:22:10,800 --> 00:22:12,720
and built environment planning is.

348
00:22:12,720 --> 00:22:17,160
We know there's lots of different ways that we can affect speed carbonization.

349
00:22:17,160 --> 00:22:19,560
This is what this article argues.

350
00:22:19,560 --> 00:22:23,040
I think said that that means planners have to reconceptualize our role

351
00:22:23,160 --> 00:22:25,840
in planning cities and cities in the energy system.

352
00:22:26,200 --> 00:22:27,640
So I hope you enjoy this lecture.

353
00:22:27,640 --> 00:22:30,840
I hope you take this class or I hope you watch the subscript videos

354
00:22:30,840 --> 00:22:35,640
on why cities are a key venue for future climate action.

